## Project layout

- `{{ django_project_slug }}/` — Django settings, URL root, WSGI/ASGI entry points{% if include_celery %}, and the Celery app{% endif %}.
- `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/` — the first Django app: models, serializers, views, urls,
  tests. New apps follow the same `apps/<name>/` layout and are added to `INSTALLED_APPS`
  as `apps.<name>`.
- Database: **{{ database }}**{% if database == 'postgres' %}, running as a sibling container
  (`db`) started automatically with the devcontainer via `docker-compose.yml`. It is reachable
  at hostname `db:5432`; `DATABASE_URL` in `.env` (copy it from `.env.example`) already points
  Django at it{% endif %}.
{% if include_celery %}
- Background tasks run through Celery, with Redis as broker/result backend, running as a
  sibling container (`redis`, reachable at `redis:6379`) started with the devcontainer.
{% endif %}

## Running things

- `uv run python manage.py runserver` — dev server.
- `uv run python manage.py makemigrations` / `migrate` — schema changes.
- `uv run pytest` — test suite (uses SQLite automatically unless `DATABASE_URL` is set, so it
  runs without any external service).
{% if use_compose %}
- The `{% if database == 'postgres' %}db{% endif %}{% if database == 'postgres' and include_celery %}/{% endif %}{% if include_celery %}redis{% endif %}` service{% if database == 'postgres' and include_celery %}s{% endif %} start and stop together with the devcontainer — you do not run `docker` yourself. They live on the compose network and are not on the host filesystem.
{% endif %}
